          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Is I10 a complete code, or does it need a fourth character?",
          "answer": "The ICD-10 code I10 is a complete code. It represents essential or primary hypertension and does not require additional characters to be billable. Most ICD-10-CM codes typically need a fourth, fifth, or sixth character for billing, but I10 is unusual because it is sufficient as a three-character code (source: \"Coding hypertension: I10 and the hypertensive disease family\").\n\nIn terms of coding conventions, ICD-10 codes can range from three to seven characters. The first character is a letter, the second is a digit, and the remaining characters may be either. A decimal point appears only when there are additional characters following the first three. For example, I10 does not have a decimal, whereas codes like E11.9 do (source: \"ICD-10-CM code structure and the conventions that govern selection\"). The three-character version of the code represents a general category, while additional characters (if present) would add specificity regarding the cause, site, or severity of the condition (source: \"ICD-10-CM code structure and the conventions that govern selection\"). \n\nHence, I10 is indeed a complete and standalone code.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
